    "content": "# Birth Preparation, Labor Stages, and Hospital Checklist\n\nPlanning for labor and delivery helps you and your birth partner feel organized and confident.\n\n## The Three Stages of Labor\n1. **First Stage (Cervical Dilation):**\n   - *Latent Phase:* The cervix softens, thins (effaces), and dilates up to 4\u20135 cm. Contractions may be irregular or spaced 5 to 20 minutes apart.\n   - *Active Phase:* Contractions become stronger, longer (lasting 60 seconds), and occur every 3 to 4 minutes. Dilation advances from 6 cm to 10 cm (full dilation).\n2. **Second Stage (Birth of the Baby):**\n   - Full cervical dilation to delivery. The mother expels the baby through coordinated contractions and maternal pushing efforts.\n3. **Third Stage (Delivery of Placenta):**\n   - The placenta and fetal membranes separate from the uterine wall and are delivered, usually within 5 to 30 minutes.\n\n## Hospital Bag Checklist Essentials\n- **Documents:** Antenatal care record/notes, photo identification, hospital registration cards, printed birth preferences.\n- **For the Mother:**\n  - 2\u20133 comfortable nightgowns or oversized tees suitable for breastfeeding and skin-to-skin contact.\n  - 5\u20136 pairs of high-waisted cotton or disposable underwear.\n  - 2 packs of absorbent postpartum/maternity sanitary pads.\n  - Non-skid slippers, warm socks, lip balm, toiletry bag, and long phone charging cables.\n  - Nursing bras and breast pads.\n- **For the Baby:**\n  - 3\u20134 newborn or 0-3 month sleepsuits (onesies) and vests.\n  - Soft baby blanket, newborn scratch mittens, and hat.\n  - 1 pack of newborn diapers and fragrance-free water wipes.\n  - Approved rear-facing infant car seat properly installed for departure.",
