    "reviewStatus": "reviewed",
    "content": "# The Menopause Transition: Perimenopause, Menopause, and Postmenopause\n\nMenopause is a natural biological milestone that reflects the gradual transition of ovarian follicle activity and hormone production.\n\n## The Three Stages\n\n### 1. Perimenopause (The Transition)\n- **Hormonal Dynamics:** As ovarian follicular reserve declines, follicle stimulating hormone (FSH) rises, causing erratic bursts of estrogen followed by sharp declines, and irregular or anovulatory cycles.\n- **Timing:** Typically begins in a woman's 40s (average onset age 47) and lasts between 4 and 8 years.\n- **Presenting Characteristics:** Changes in cycle frequency (cycles may shorten to 21 days or lengthen to 60+ days), heavier or lighter flow, sleep changes, hot flashes, and mood shifts.\n\n### 2. Menopause (The Milestone)\n- **Clinical Definition:** Diagnosed retrospectively after **12 consecutive months of amenorrhea** (absence of periods) without other pathological or physiological causes.\n- **Timing:** The average age of natural menopause globally is 51 years.\n\n### 3. Postmenopause (Long-Term Health)\n- **Hormonal Dynamics:** Ovaries produce very low levels of estradiol, though the adrenal glands and peripheral adipose tissue continue to convert androgens into estrone.\n- **Focus of Care:** Long-term cardiovascular protection, bone density preservation (osteopenia/osteoporosis prevention), and genitourinary health.",
