    "reviewStatus": "reviewed",
    "content": "# Period Tracking and Irregular Menstrual Cycles\n\nTracking your cycle establishes what is normal for you and provides objective information for your healthcare provider.\n\n## What is Considered a Regular Cycle?\n- **Cycle Frequency:** 21 to 35 days (from Day 1 of one period to Day 1 of the subsequent period). In adolescent years, cycles may range up to 45 days.\n- **Cycle Regularity:** Cycle-to-cycle variation of less than 7 to 9 days across a 12-month span.\n- **Bleeding Duration:** 3 to 7 days of active flow.\n\n## Common Causes of Irregularity\n1. **Life Transitions:** Cycles are naturally more variable in the first few years after menarche (first period) and during perimenopause due to fluctuating ovulation patterns.\n2. **Stress & Cortisol:** Elevated physiological or emotional stress alters hypothalamic gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) signaling, delaying or inhibiting ovulation.\n3. **Weight Fluctuations & High Intensity Training:** Significant caloric deficits or rapid body composition changes can affect hormone production.\n4. **Endocrine Conditions:** Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) and thyroid disorders (hypo- or hyperthyroidism) are common clinical causes of irregular ovulation.\n5. **Medication & Contraception:** Starting, stopping, or using hormonal contraceptives or emergency contraception frequently changes flow patterns.\n\n## How Tracking Helps\nRecording the first day of bleeding, flow volume, and daily symptoms creates a longitudinal trend summary. Instead of guessing during clinical appointments, you can share concrete counts and cycle intervals.",
