    "content": "# When to Seek Medical Care for Menstrual Symptoms\n\nWhile moderate discomfort and minor schedule shifts can occur naturally, certain symptoms warrant professional medical attention to ensure your well-being.\n\n## Urgent Warning Signs (Seek Immediate Medical Care)\nPlease go to an emergency room or contact urgent medical services immediately if you experience:\n- **Excessive, Rapid Blood Loss:** Soaking through two or more heavy pads or tampons every hour for 2 consecutive hours.\n- **Signs of Acute Hypovolemia/Anemia:** Dizziness, lightheadedness, sudden pale skin, fainting, shortness of breath, or rapid heartbeat.\n- **Severe, Sudden Pelvic Pain:** Severe acute pelvic or abdominal pain, especially if accompanied by high fever, vomiting, or chills.\n- **Possible Pregnancy Complication:** Severe unilateral pelvic cramping or heavy bleeding if pregnancy is possible (to rule out ectopic pregnancy).\n\n## Non-Urgent Indicators for Clinical Consultation\nSchedule a consultation with your gynecologist or primary care physician if you notice:\n- **Prolonged Bleeding:** Periods regularly lasting longer than 7 to 8 days.\n- **Irregular Intervals:** Cycles consistently shorter than 21 days or longer than 35 days, or sudden cessation of periods for 3+ months (secondary amenorrhea).\n- **Intermenstrual or Post-coital Bleeding:** Bleeding or spotting between periods or following sexual intercourse.\n- **Debilitating Pain:** Period pain that prevents you from going to work, school, or daily activities, or does not respond to standard pain relievers.\n- **Large Blood Clots:** Consistently passing blood clots larger than 2.5 cm (approximately the size of a quarter).",
